    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Wherever your hand stops, the notes under it belong to two windows at once. You are never between shapes."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Now open the "
        },
        {
          "text": "Scale Visualizer",
          "link": {
            "kind": "screen",
            "href": "/scale-visualizer"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": ", set the root to "
        },
        {
          "text": "A",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and the scale to minor pentatonic, and there are two position systems on offer: CAGED and Boxes. That matters. Spend a whole chapter on CAGED, hit the other toggle, and you will see five windows in different places under different names and conclude that one of the two must be wrong. "
        },
        {
          "text": "Neither is.",
          "marks": ["bold"]
        },
        {
          "text": " Worth knowing: Boxes only appears for a five-note scale — set the scale to natural minor and the second toggle becomes something else entirely."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Before anything else about the two systems, this: the dots do not move. Both toggles draw the same notes on the same neck; what changes is where the frames are drawn and what they're called. It's the same idea chapter 1 used for C major and A minor — the dots stay put, the labels move — now applied to two ways of framing one scale."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "tone": "info",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "The dots do not move; the labels do."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Why they differ is worth deriving rather than just accepting. Both systems are five windows placed relative to the fret where the root sits on the low "
        },
        {
          "text": "E",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " string — fret "
        },
        {
          "text": "5",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " for A. CAGED's windows sit where the five chord shapes put their barre: "
        },
        {
          "text": "0, 2, 4, 7, 9",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " frets above that root. Boxes sit on the scale itself: "
        },
        {
          "text": "0, 3, 5, 7, 10",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " frets above that root — that's "
        },
        {
          "text": "1 b3 4 5 b7",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", the minor pentatonic walked up the low "
        },
        {
          "text": "E",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " string. On the low "
        },
        {
          "text": "E",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " in A minor that's frets "
        },
        {
          "text": "0, 3, 5, 8, 10",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and then "
        },
        {
          "text": "12",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", and those are exactly where the boxes start."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Two of those five offsets are the same in both lists — "
        },
        {
          "text": "0",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and "
        },
        {
          "text": "7",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". That's why exactly two of the five pairs line up."
        }
      ]
    },
