    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "One relationship holds across all five: every box's dots are a subset of its CAGED partner's, checked position by position. A box is never wider than its form and never holds a dot the form doesn't. It's the form with its bottom edge trimmed — and, for Box 3, its top edge as well."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "live",
      "component": "caged-ladder",
      "props": {
        "root": "A",
        "quality": "minor"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "The ladder marks roots only, whatever the layer — there's no "
        },
        {
          "text": "4",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " or "
        },
        {
          "text": "b7",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " to find on it. It's here to show the CAGED tiling whole — the five bands it draws are exactly the CAGED column in the table above."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "\"Box 1 at fret "
        },
        {
          "text": "5",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": "\" — the thing half the guitar-playing world learns first — "
        },
        {
          "text": "is the E form.",
          "marks": ["bold"]
        },
        {
          "text": " Same twelve dots, checked position by position; the box just doesn't bother drawing fret "
        },
        {
          "text": "4",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", which holds nothing anyway. That's "
        },
        {
          "text": "the window from the previous lesson",
          "link": {
            "kind": "article",
            "slug": "minor-caged-pentatonic-e-form"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": ", under a different name. Box 4, the open-position box, is the A form for the same reason at the other end: the nut trims the form to exactly the box's span."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Here's the trap. Going up the neck from the nut in A minor, the boxes read "
        },
        {
          "text": "4, 5, 1, 2, 3",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". Box 1 is not the lowest box on the neck — it's the one that starts on the root on the low "
        },
        {
          "text": "E",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " string. So \"box 1\" and \"the first form\" are not the same idea, and a player who assumes they are will be two windows out."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "tone": "warning",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Box numbering does not start at the nut. Box 1 is the one rooted on the low "
        },
        {
          "text": "E",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " string, which for A minor is fret "
        },
        {
          "text": "5",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": "."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Which system to use is an honest question, not a trick one: neither is more correct, and both draw the same notes. CAGED is the one this pathway uses because it's the same five windows that carry the chords — the reason you can see which dots are chord tones at all. If you already knew the boxes, you haven't wasted anything: you have been playing these same five windows all along — two of them exactly, the other three with an edge trimmed off."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "One more note gets a name before this chapter ends, and only a name: between the "
        },
        {
          "text": "4",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and the "
        },
        {
          "text": "5",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " sits the "
        },
        {
          "text": "b5",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", players slide through it on the way from one to the other, it is not in this scale, and this pathway does not teach it any further than that."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Chapter 4 takes the same five windows and adds two more notes — still no new shapes. Flip between the two toggles in the "
        },
        {
          "text": "Scale Visualizer",
          "link": {
            "kind": "screen",
            "href": "/scale-visualizer"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " and watch the dots stay put while the frames rearrange, or hold a sustained "
        },
        {
          "text": "A",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " on the "
        },
        {
          "text": "Drone",
          "link": {
            "kind": "screen",
            "href": "/drone"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " and play any window from this chapter against it."
        }
      ]
    }
